    {
      "episode_id": "utxo-commitments",
      "proposal": "UTXO commitments vs AssumeUTXO",
      "venue": "bitcoin-dev",
      "technical_objections": [
        {
          "text": "A soft-fork to make (U)TXO commitments always required is problematic, because we can't change the format of those commitments in the future in another soft-fork - the exact form is baked in stone.",
          "source": "github",
          "answered": "",
          "basis": "",
          "cite": "3ec77c6ca322a8075b81313746314f3dbb4b668a9dafccebbc97c7741a033855"
        },
        {
          "text": "The trouble is this requires maintaining a hash tree commitment over validator state, which turns out to be insanely expensive. \u2026 that ends up requiring 15 - 22x more I/O during block validation.",
          "source": "mail",
          "answered": "",
          "basis": "",
          "cite": "6f6f7d58549247ea96a42ed22c5156367d6590ee67f0aa028639caada3f2f9bd"
        },
        {
          "text": "My objection is that `assumeUTXO` makes the most critical part of the codebase harder to read and reason about, and this PR extends that model further.",
          "source": "github",
          "answered": "",
          "basis": "",
          "cite": "5074841b738fea4e44bd8331bfcc281a2cc78dcbcfe001a745d2debe9842f431"
        }
      ],
      "process_objections": [],
      "outcome": "",
